120 runs. Three questions, asked of the same data.

1. What is wrong: 6 failures, by population.

   document          3 of 74      4% of that population
   unanswerable      2 of 20     10% of that population
   warehouse         1 of 20      5% of that population

   Sort by rate, not by count. document produces the most failures because it is
   the largest population; unanswerable fails at 10%, which is the highest rate and
   therefore the population to work on. It is 20 cases, so a fix aimed
   at it is a fix you can measure — and a fix aimed at 'accuracy' is not.

2. What is slow: the five slowest, against a median of 3.2s.

   tail-rotated-util       11.0s   8,610 tokens  document
   abstain-17               9.7s   6,825 tokens  unanswerable
   abstain-09               9.0s   5,087 tokens  unanswerable
   abstain-19               8.9s   3,571 tokens  unanswerable
   tail-three-months        8.7s   5,959 tokens  document

   The slowest is 3.5x the median. Tail latency is not an average
   problem: it is a small number of runs doing much more work, and §17.9's
   budget is the lever that bounds them.

3. What to add to the eval set.

   8 cases earned a place: every failure, every wrong refusal,
   and the three slowest. They are already written — that is the point of
   triaging a run rather than inventing cases at a whiteboard.

     6 failures            they are regressions the moment they are fixed
     2 wrong refusals      the most expensive failure mode to leave in
     3 slowest runs        a latency budget needs cases that test it
     3 on two lists        counted once in the total above

   And one thing the card does not show: the most expensive answer used 9.9x
   the tokens of the median one (8,610 against 870). A mean cost per
   answer hides that entirely, which is why §28.1 measures cost per request rather
   than per month.

That is the loop. Ship, measure, triage the failures into the set, fix the
worst-rate population, re-measure against the card. Every step of it is
evidence, and none of it needs anybody to have an opinion about what users want.
